{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjYuZG2fBy2MLcv8QZEd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WVM2PhWXtvDLKaz8eSvVMy","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line has five bars, separated by four barlines.\n\nBar 1 is edition bar 28. It opens with a grace note and a run of 32nds: G F G Ab. Next come 16ths, Bb G Bb G. Then a grace note leads to 32nds descending to Eb (F Eb F G) and 16ths Ab F Ab F. The small mark under the start looks like the [pp].\n\nBar 2 is bar 29. It starts with a 32nd figure rising from G: G Ab Bb C. Then come 16ths Bb G Bb G, a grace Bb to Ab, 32nds F Eb F G, and 16ths Ab F Ab F.\n\nBar 3 is bar 30. It is four beamed groups of 16ths. The first two are G–Bb alternations and the last two are lower Eb–Bb alternations.\n\nBar 4 is bar 31: a quarter on Eb4, a quarter rest, then a half note Ab4 with its flat.\n\nBar 5 is bar 32. It has a beamed G4 8th plus a dotted G–F figure, then a quarter Eb4 tied to an Eb4 16th. A high beamed group follows (Eb5, E5, F5), then a flagged F4 8th and its rest at the right edge.\n\nBar 32 is complete and the line ends there. Nothing from bar 33 appears: there is no 16th rest followed by D5 D5 Eb5. So the line holds bars 28–32, not 28–33.","right":false,"insights":"The first four bars were easy to match because their rhythms are distinctive: 32nd runs, repeated 16th alternations, and quarter–rest–half. The hard part was whether the last bar ends the line or runs into bar 33, because the right edge is crowded and has no closing barline. A clearer right margin, or knowing the first bar of the next line, would have settled it."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6178,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6178},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier